  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION, MARINE, AND BEACHES DISCUSSIONS...\n\nPatchy stratus has developed across San Diego County this morning\nand a band of scattered high clouds is beginning to move in from\nthe northwest. At the surface, pressure gradients are trending\noffshore - currently around -5.3 mb SAN-TPH and -1.6 mb SAN-DAG.\nWinds are gusting 20-30 mph in the Cajon Pass but remain weak\nelsewhere. Weak offshore flow will persist through the late\nmorning followed by a weak sea breeze this afternoon. Otherwise\nwarmer today with highs near normal and scattered high clouds\nthrough the day. \n\nAn upper level short-wave trough moving inland across the Pac NW\nthis morning will dig southeast into the Great Basin later today\nbefore a closed low develops in the base of the trough over\nArizona on Friday. This will drive stronger offshore pressure\ngradients and provide a bit of upper level support as\nnortheasterly mid-level flow develops over the region on the back\nside of the low. The EC ensemble remains stronger with forecast\ngradients than the GEFS, however the GEFS mean is much closer to\nobserved gradients this morning. Expect Santa Ana winds to\nincrease on Friday but generally be confined to the wind-prone\npasses, canyons, and coastal slopes/foothills. Gradients continue\nto strengthen on Saturday, especially to the east, though we lose\nsome of the upper level support as the upper low shifts south and\neast. Winds both days are forecast to gust to around 25-40 mph,\nfirst focused in the Cajon Pass/Santa Ana Mountains on Friday as\nwinds are northeasterly, then the San Gorgonio Pass/San Diego\nCounty Mountains on Saturday as winds turn more easterly.\nGradients and winds weaken for Sunday and Monday, eventually\nturning back onshore towards the middle of next week. \n\nWith offshore winds and high pressure building in from the west,\nexpect much warmer weather into Sunday, especially west of the\nmountains. By Friday, highs will be as much as 5-10 degrees above\nnormal for the western valleys and coastal areas (highs in the low\nto mid 80s), but remain near to slightly below normal for the\nmountains and deserts due to cold air advection from the north.\nFor this weekend, highs will be closer to 7-15 degrees above\nnormal for the coastal areas and valleys, and near to around 3\ndegrees above normal elsewhere. Highs could push the 90 degree\nmark on Sunday. \n\nThe upper level ridge weakens and a cut-off low passes by to the\nsouth Monday and Tuesday allowing for slow cooling. An upper level\nlow moves towards the West Coast on Wednesday, though depending on\nits position we could see more cooling (further east position), or\nstill be under a ridge with little cooling (further west\nposition). The progression of this trough will have a larger\ninfluence on the weather late in the week, not only in terms of\nhow much cooling, but precipitation chances as well. For now there\nis a slight (15-20%) chance beginning on Thursday, though\nsignificant spread exists in the ensembles regarding the timing\nand amounts for late in the week. It looks like that will also\nbegin a fairly active weather pattern, and the CPC 8-14 day\noutlook is showing a 60-70% chance of above normal rainfall along\nthe entire CA coast.",
